window.docContents[241].resolve({"/UD-IV___-abstraction/Lambdas-and-function-values/#Core-C______--UD-IV___-abstraction--Lambdas-and-function-values":{"contents":"A lambda has no type of its own. The judgment ,  is acceptable at , checks a lambda against the std::function type expected where it occurs, as argument, as initialiser of a declaration and as return expression, and for any other expression it is  with . The body is checked under  with every variable of the enclosing scope marked read only, the copies of [=], and with the parameters of the lambda as ordinary variables. The rule T-LocVar requires a variable that is not read only, so an assignment to a captured variable, or a reference to it, is a type error.Outside its three positions a lambda is a type error, and auto x = [=]… is a syntax error, because the grammar admits a lambda only as an argument expression.\n\nA lambda evaluates to a closure with copies of the free variables of its body that the environment binds, taken when the lambda is evaluated. The closure holds values, not locations. A captured int or bool is a copy the body reads and never writes, and a captured pointer still reaches its object in , so an effect through it is visible outside the lambda.\n\nA call through a function value evaluates the function expression to a closure, the arguments by value and left to right, allocates fresh locations for the captured copies and for the parameters, runs the body in an environment with those bindings only, and frees them on return. When the callee is a variable f of function type, f(…) is this rule and not the call of the function named f.With  in place of  the result is  if  and error otherwise. Nothing of the environment of the call is visible inside the body, only the copies and the parameters.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009UD IV, abstraction","header":"5.3. Lambdas and function values","id":"/UD-IV___-abstraction/Lambdas-and-function-values/#Core-C______--UD-IV___-abstraction--Lambdas-and-function-values"}});